# clause — backlog

> **Type-surface reminder**: new entries use `arvo::USize` /
> `arvo::UFixed<N, 0, S>` instead of `usize` / `u*`,
> `notko::Maybe` / `notko::Outcome` instead of `Option` /
> `Result`, `hilavitkutin_str::Str` instead of `String` or
> non-static `&str`. See `.claude/rules/type-surface.md`.

Items decided but deliberately out of the round-one shipping
scope. Each lands as its own follow-up design round.

## parse subcommand — real AST printer

The round-one stub prints a phase placeholder. The real
implementation pretty-prints the AST via a
`vehje-syntax`-owned printer once the parser productions
ship. Output format echoes the shape of the stellar-heritage
Python oracle's `clause parse --json` but stays human-readable
by default.

## check subcommand — real typecheck

Depends on `vehje-typecheck` validator bodies. Prints every
diagnostic the registry emits for the input program, sorted
by file + offset. Exit code `1` on any emitted diagnostic.

## build subcommand — target dispatch

Takes a `--target <name>` flag, dispatches to a
`CodegenTarget` implementation, writes emitted bytes to
stdout or a `--out <file>` path. Depends on real `vehje-
codegen` target bodies.

## run subcommand — compile + interpret

Compiles through the front end, passes the emitted bytecode
to an embedded interpreter. Depends on an interpreter design
round (not yet opened).

## Pass-DAG wiring via vehje-schedule

Replaces the hard-coded phase chain in each subcommand with
a graph walk through `vehje-schedule` once that crate ships
a pass DAG. Round-one subcommands stay hard-coded; the
swap happens when `vehje-schedule` offers a real entry
point.

## Workspace manifest parsing

Round one takes a single `<file>` input. Real projects are
multi-file with a `Vehje.toml` manifest. The manifest round
introduces module-graph loading, edition selection, target
list, dependency resolution. Parser lives in
`vehje-resolve::manifest` today as a stub; the binary
invokes it once it is real.

## Argument parser library swap

The hand-rolled `args.rs` is fine for five subcommands with
one positional each. If the CLI grows beyond that (flags per
subcommand, output formatters, verbosity levels, `--help`
generation), swap to `argh` or `clap`. Criterion: `args.rs`
exceeds ~120 lines or requires more than one parser per
subcommand family.

## Script-mode entry

`clause <file>` as a shorthand for `clause run <file>` so the
binary works as a `#!`-invoked interpreter for `.clause`
scripts. Depends on `run` landing first.

## Exit code table freeze

Round-one freezes:

- `0` — success.
- `1` — phase-level failure (lex diagnostic, parse error,
  typecheck diagnostic, codegen error).
- `2` — stub / not-yet-implemented or I/O failure.

Script authors against the binary may rely on these codes.
Future rounds that widen the surface add new codes from `3`
upward; they do not repurpose `0`, `1`, `2`.
